  "notes": "Full RSS capture, 79 processes with nonzero VmRSS (captured via /proc/<pid>/status on the live device; the capture script's own process is excluded from this list). System-level used memory (MemTotal - MemAvailable, per Section 3.5 MEM-006 guidance) is 905.6 MiB \u2014 up from 573-631 MiB in earlier snapshots, tracking the onewifi_em_ctrl/onewifi_em_agent growth below. totalRssMB (1053.06) is the GROSS sum of listed processes and double-counts shared pages across processes; it does not equal the 905.6 MiB system-level figure. This capture shows no netdata, ollama, or apps.plugin \u2014 the earlier capture's dev-only tooling \u2014 confirming this is a cleaner, more production-representative image than the original baseline. mariadbd is still present (still not normative for either in-scope profile). \n\nThree-point growth history for the two flagged processes, in chronological order: onewifi_em_ctrl 118.7 MiB \u2192 236.9 MiB \u2192 271.9 MiB (this capture); onewifi_em_agent 21.0 MiB \u2192 64.2 MiB \u2192 215.4 MiB (this capture), the last point now over 10x its original value and larger than OneWifi itself. Three consecutive increases is materially stronger evidence toward a genuine leak than the earlier two-point 'growth vs. leak, undistinguished' framing \u2014 this has not been reclassified as a confirmed leak, since exact device uptime at each capture is not recorded and the formal 72-hour soak test (MEM-008 criterion) still has not been run, but this pattern warrants prioritizing that soak test. \n\nA handful of process names could not be independently de-truncated from this capture's 15-character-limited /proc/<pid>/status Name field and are recorded as truncated: rdkbLogMonitor. (likely rdkbLogMonitor.sh), self_heal_conne, resource_monito, ntp-data-collec, syseventd_fork_, netlink-button-. Names confirmed against the earlier full-name capture (onewifi_em_agent, telcovoice_manager, systemd-journald, fwupgrademanager, hal-voice-asterisk, selfheal_aggressive) have been corrected to their full spelling here."
